2026-06-30 15:20:32,780 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-ce7feb7e-ec72-4975-b5d1-69ea4beaa511', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert proteomics assistant. Return valid JSON only.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'You are a proteomics expert analyzing crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nYour task is to cluster raw spectral files into biologically meaningful groups.\n\nA cluster should represent a unique combination of:\n1. Biological / experimental condition\n   - Examples: treatment, control, carbon source, growth medium, cell state, tissue, disease state, time point, perturbation, purified complex, recombinant standard set.\n2. Source organism of the proteome being analyzed\n   - Return the full scientific name.\n   - Use the organism of the biological sample or proteome of interest, not necessarily the expression host.\n   - Example: if human proteins are expressed in E. coli and analyzed as human recombinant standards, the source organism is Homo sapiens.\n   - If the dataset intentionally contains multiple source organisms, create separate clusters by organism when possible.\n3. Crosslinking reagent\n   - Examples: BS3, DSS, DSSO, DSBU, formaldehyde.\n   - If multiple crosslinkers are used, create separate clusters by reagent when possible.\n\nDo NOT split clusters by:\n- fraction number\n- LC fraction\n- technical replicate\n- biological replicate ID\n- injection number\n- file numbering\n- enrichment fraction\n- instrument run order\n- search batch\n- calibration/QC files\n\nReplicates, fractions, and repeated injections from the same biological condition should be grouped together.\n\n---\n\nINPUTS\n\nRaw filenames:\n20201023_OR16_UM7_Hevle001_SA_HTTQ23HAP40_PhoX_E1.raw\n20201023_OR16_UM7_Hevle001_SA_HTTQ23HAP40_PhoX_E2.raw\n20201023_OR16_UM7_Hevle001_SA_HTTQ23HAP40_PhoX_E3.raw\n20201023_OR16_UM7_Hevle001_SA_HTTQ23HAP40_PhoX_E4.raw\n20210210_EX2_UM3_hevle001_SA_HTTQ54HAP40_PhoX_E1.raw\n20210210_EX2_UM3_hevle001_SA_HTTQ54HAP40_PhoX_E2.raw\n20210210_EX2_UM3_hevle001_SA_HTTQ54HAP40_PhoX_E3.raw\n20210210_EX2_UM3_hevle001_SA_HTTQ54HAP40_PhoX_E4.raw\n20210210_EX2_UM3_hevle001_SA_HTTdEx1HAP40_PhoX_E1.raw\n20210210_EX2_UM3_hevle001_SA_HTTdEx1HAP40_PhoX_E2.raw\n20210210_EX2_UM3_hevle001_SA_HTTdEx1HAP40_PhoX_E3.raw\n20210210_EX2_UM3_hevle001_SA_HTTdEx1HAP40_PhoX_E4.raw\n\nPublication text and PRIDE metadata:\n[TITLE]\nHAP40 orchestrates huntingtin structure for differential interaction with polyglutamine expanded exon 1\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nHuntington’s disease results from expansion of a glutamine-coding CAG tract in the huntingtin (HTT) gene, producing an aberrantly functioning form of HTT. Both wildtype and disease-state HTT form a hetero-dimer with HAP40 of unknown functional relevance. We demonstrate in vivo and in cell models that HTT and HAP40 cellular abundance are coupled. Integrating data from a 2.6 Å cryo-electron microscopy structure, cross-linking mass spectrometry, small-angle X-ray scattering, and modeling, we provide a near-atomic-level view of HTT, its molecular interaction surfaces and compacted domain architecture, orchestrated by HAP40. Native mass-spectrometry reveals a remarkably stable hetero-dimer, potentially explaining the cellular inter-dependence of HTT and HAP40. The exon 1 region of HTT is dynamic but shows greater conformational variety in the polyglutamine expanded mutant than wildtype exon 1. Our data provide a foundation for future functional and drug discovery studies targeting Huntington’s disease and illuminate the structural consequences of HTT polyglutamine expansion.\n\n[METHODS]\nCross-linking of HTT-HAP40 samples with PhoX For cross-linking experiments, HTT-HAP40 samples (HTTQ23-HAP40, HTTQ54-HAP40, HTT Δexon 1-HAP40) were diluted to a protein concentration of 1 mg/1 mL using cross-linking buffer (20 mM Hepes pH 7.4, 300 mM NaCl, 2.5 % glycerol, 1 mM TCEP). HTT-HAP40 samples were treated with an optimised concentration of PhoX cross-linker to avoid protein aggregation (Supplementary Figure 4a). After incubation with PhoX (0.5 mM) for 30 min at RT, the reaction was quenched for additional 30 min at RT by the addition of Tris HCl (1 M, pH 7.5) to a final concentration of 50 mM. Protein digestion was performed in 100 mM Tris-HCl, pH 8.5, 1 % SDC, 5 mM TCEP and 30 mM CAA, with the addition of Lys-C and Trypsin proteases (1:25 and 1:100 ratio (w/w)) overnight at 37 °C. The reaction was stopped by addition of TFA to a final concentration of 0.1 % or until pH ~ 2. Next, peptides were desalted using an Oasis HLB plate, before IMAC enrichment of cross-linked peptides like previously described. Four technical replicates were completed for each form of HTT-HAP40.  For LC-MS analysis, the samples were re-suspended in 2 % formic acid and analyzed using an UltiMate™ 3000 RSLCnano System (Thermo Fischer Scientific) coupled on-line to either a Q Exactive HF-X (Thermo Fischer Scientific), or an Orbitrap Exploris 480 (Thermo Fischer Scientific). Firstly, peptides were trapped for 5 min in solvent A (0.1 % FA in water), using a 100-µm inner diameter 2-cm trap column (packed in-house with ReproSil-Pur C18-AQ, 3 µm) prior to separation on an analytical column (50 cm of length, 75 µM inner diameter; packed in-house with Poroshell 120 EC-C18, 2.7 µm). Peptides were eluted following a 45 or 55 min gradient from 9-35 % solvent B (80 % ACN, 0.1 % FA), respectively 9- 41 % solvent B. On the Q Exactive HF-X a full scan MS spectra from 375-1600 Da were acquired in the Orbitrap at a resolution of 60,000 with the AGC target set to 3 x 106 and maximum injection time of 120 ms. For measurements on the Orbitrap Exploris 480, a full scan MS spectra from 375-2200 m/z were acquired in the Orbitrap at a resolution of 60,000 with the AGC target set to 2 x 106 and maximum injection time of 25 ms. Only peptides with charged states 3-8 were fragmented, and dynamic exclusion properties were set to n = 1, for a duration of 10 s (Q Exactive HF-X), respectively 15 s (Orbitrap Exploris 480). Fragmentation was performed using in a stepped HCD collision energy mode (27, 30, 33 % Q Exactive HF-X; 20, 28, 36 % Orbitrap Exploris 480) in the ion trap and acquired in the Orbitrap at a resolution of 30,000 after accumulating a target value of 1 x 105 with an isolation window of 1.4 m/z and maximum injection time of 54 ms (Q Exactive HF-X), respectively 55 ms Orbitrap Exploris 480.\n\n\nPreviously extracted organism(s) and NCBI taxid(s):\nHomo sapiens (human)\n\n---\n\nTASKS\n\n1. Identify the distinct biological/experimental conditions described by the publication, PRIDE metadata, and filenames.\n\n2. Assign every raw file to exactly one cluster.\n\n3. Define clusters using the smallest number of biologically meaningful groups. Do not over-cluster based only on replicate, fraction, or run-number differences.\n\n4. For each cluster, report:\n   - cluster_id: "cluster_1", "cluster_2", etc.\n   - cluster_label: short machine-readable label using lowercase words separated by underscores\n   - description: concise human-readable summary of the condition\n   - organism: full scientific name of the proteome/sample organism\n   - taxid: NCBI Taxonomy CURIE, e.g. "NCBITaxon:9606"; use null if unknown\n   - crosslinker: crosslinking reagent; use null if unknown\n   - biological_condition: short phrase describing the treatment/control/state\n   - files: list of raw filenames assigned to this cluster\n   - confidence: "high", "medium", or "low"\n   - evidence_quote: exact sentence or short phrase from the publication, PRIDE metadata, or filename pattern that supports this cluster assignment\n   - reasoning: brief explanation of why these files belong together\n\n5. Assign files that cannot be confidently interpreted to cluster_0 with:\n   - cluster_label: "unassigned"\n   - confidence: "low"\n   - evidence_quote: null\n   - reasoning: explain why assignment was not possible\n\n6. QC, calibrant, blank, wash, or test files should be assigned to cluster_0 unless the publication clearly states they are part of the biological experiment.\n\n7. If all biological files appear to come from one condition, create only cluster_1 plus cluster_0 if there are unassigned/QC files.\n\n8. Confidence guidelines:\n   - "high": filenames and publication/metadata agree clearly\n   - "medium": assignment is likely but partially inferred from filenames or incomplete metadata\n   - "low": weak evidence, ambiguous naming, missing metadata, or conflicting information\n\n9. If publication text and filenames conflict, prioritize the publication/PRIDE metadata, but mention the conflict in reasoning.\n\n10. Return ONLY valid JSON. Do not include markdown, comments, or explanatory text outside the JSON.\n\n---\n\nOUTPUT JSON STRUCTURE\n\n{\n  "clusters": [\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_1",\n      "cluster_label": "yeast_glucose_bs3",\n      "description": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae samples grown in glucose-rich medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "organism": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae",\n      "taxid": "NCBITaxon:4932",\n      "crosslinker": "BS3",\n      "biological_condition": "glucose-rich medium",\n      "files": [\n        "YeastGlu_R1.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_R2.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_fraction_01.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "high",\n      "evidence_quote": "Yeast cells were cultured in glucose- or glycerol-containing medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "reasoning": "The filenames contain Glu and the publication states that glucose-grown yeast samples were crosslinked with BS3."\n    },\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_0",\n      "cluster_label": "unassigned",\n      "description": "Files that could not be confidently assigned to a biological XL-MS condition.",\n      "organism": null,\n      "taxid": null,\n      "crosslinker": null,\n      "biological_condition": null,\n      "files": [\n        "calibrant.raw",\n        "QC.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "low",\n      "evidence_quote": null,\n      "reasoning": "These files appear to be QC or calibration runs rather than biological samples."\n    }\n  ],\n  "summary": {\n    "n_input_files": 5,\n    "n_assigned_files": 3,\n    "n_unassigned_files": 2,\n    "n_biological_clusters": 1,\n    "overall_confidence": "high"\n  }\n}'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
